#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203857 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c676a83b-f3d4-579b-ab54-de2104552e8f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Gradient of a Function With Respect to a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "ca1002c8-c615-5f7b-8876-82cf87c8864f" :question/math-academy-id "e-17349" :question/problem "Consider the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(X) = (\\det(X))^2$.\n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ with respect to a matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11} & x_{12} \\\\ x_{21} & x_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X) = (\\det(X))^2 = (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^2\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{X}f(X) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x_{11}}(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂x_{12}}(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x_{21}}(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂x_{22}}(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}) \\cdot x_{22} & 2(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}) \\cdot (-x_{21}) \\\\ 2(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}) \\cdot (-x_{12}) & 2(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}) \\cdot x_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})\\begin{bmatrix}x_{22} & -x_{21} \\\\ -x_{12} & x_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2det(X)\\begin{bmatrix}x_{22} & -x_{21} \\\\ -x_{12} & x_{11}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{X}f(X) &= 2(-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10 - (-4) \\cdot \\frac{3}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2(-5 - (-6))\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}20 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203866 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7b2139-06eb-5584-a267-d9fbc9b5d041" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule to Calculate Gradients"}] :question/id #uuid "429a6a34-2fe5-5c09-9b74-e4b650209007" :question/math-academy-id "e-17870" :question/problem "Consider the function $L: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $L(\\mathbf{A}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})^T(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat is $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $\\mathbf{A}$ is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $\\mathbf{A}$.\n\nWe can compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nby applying the chain rule.\n\nLet\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\nL(\\mathbf{A}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = \\sum_{k=1}^2 \\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} L = \\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y} \\bigg) = \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} = y_k\n$$\n\nWriting out\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nin component-wise notation as\n\n$$\ny_i = \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^3 (a_{ij} x_j - b_i)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}} = x_j$ if $i = k$, and\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}} = 0$ if $i \\neq k$.\n\nThis means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂L}{∂a_{ij}} &= ∑_(k = 1)^(2)\\frac{∂L}{∂y_{k}} \\cdot \\frac{∂y_{k}}{∂a_{ij}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂L}{∂y_{i}} \\cdot \\frac{∂y_{i}}{∂a_{ij}} \\\\\n&= y_{i} \\cdot \\frac{∂y_{i}}{∂a_{ij}} \\\\\n&= y_{i}x_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ is the $i$th entry of $\\mathbf{y}$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $\\mathbf{x}$, so\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{A}L &= y \\cdot x^{T} \\\\\n&= (Ax - b) \\cdot x^{T} \\\\\n&= (\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= (\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203871 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204080 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35dc17b6-1c82-55ec-9657-20f2850794e5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Gradient of a Function Component-Wise"}] :question/id #uuid "5aa67862-6f7a-57ac-9473-d039fc4bfec4" :question/math-academy-id "e-17951" :question/problem "Consider the function $L: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $L(\\mathbf{A}) = \\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}$ where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat is $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $\\mathbf{A}$ is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $\\mathbf{A}$.\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as\n\n$$\nL = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^2 \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^3 a_{ij} y_i x_j\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = y_i x_j\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T\n$$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c676a83b-f3d4-579b-ab54-de2104552e8f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Gradient of a Function With Respect to a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a845410e-2358-5f09-af27-95edceb70e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203334} {:answer/id #uuid "daea1ca9-49fd-5cca-90fd-3470bc6e4bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203335} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc8dd0d-f884-50f2-b1ff-fc7cd48b7b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203336} {:answer/id #uuid "3d97bb17-7392-5837-b742-afba8fff1dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203337} {:answer/id #uuid "6c47d4b1-a923-5d13-9ea2-f25e49d50c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203338}] :answer-field/id #uuid "18959201-dc75-5139-b452-5ab59d2a87d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203333}] :question/id #uuid "8781dff9-d5f6-57b2-92f1-799f898568b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-264512" :question/problem "Consider the function $f: R^{2 \\cdot 2} → R$ defined by\n$f(X) =-\\ln (det(X))$.\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c676a83b-f3d4-579b-ab54-de2104552e8f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Gradient of a Function With Respect to a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8240f1b9-81d6-5f55-960f-980834ff80a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203371} {:answer/id #uuid "cb14b150-2e63-5c91-bfa3-5c9d564562a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203372} {:answer/id #uuid "278659cc-6712-578c-acd1-dc0b9022ced4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203373} {:answer/id #uuid "43b869e1-ce1b-59c7-8f2f-7fd6649bffdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203374} {:answer/id #uuid "510ddf5f-6414-51fa-8c1f-656228ec753b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 16 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203375}] :answer-field/id #uuid "006b5aba-a8d4-5887-b052-f91f00560b87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203370}] :question/id #uuid "52069b73-e5e5-5970-bfc8-fea272282f4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-265775" :question/problem "Consider the function $f: R^{2 \\cdot 2} → R$ defined by\n$f(X) = ∑_(i = 1)^(2)∑_(j = 1)^(2)x_{ij}^{2}$\nwhere $x_{ij}$ are the corresponding matrix's entries. What is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7b2139-06eb-5584-a267-d9fbc9b5d041" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule to Calculate Gradients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "95ff86ad-4fd6-5245-9c4f-f316fd454074" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203438} {:answer-field/id #uuid "b9c65c04-6b53-5f85-a4b7-016b67a85bbe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203439} {:answer-field/id #uuid "62e2c4dd-822a-5e84-94f2-df21dbbd5c71" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203440}] :question/id #uuid "add77669-c349-54f8-bd8c-094573c46893" :question/math-academy-id "q-269590" :question/problem "Consider the function $L: R^{3 \\cdot 2} → R$ defined by $L(A) = (3Ax - 2b)^{T}(3Ax - 2b)$, where\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat is $∇_{A}L$ at\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$?\n\n| $∇_{A} =$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-96$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $0$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} | $864$ |  |  |"}]}
